{% doc %}
  A bare previous/next pair for a `<swiper-carousel>`.

  Use `carousel-controls` when you want the numbers and the progress bar too;
  this is the same buttons with nothing between them.

  ## Placement

  Arrows do not always belong inside the carousel. A section header often wants
  them beside the heading, a hero wants them over the image, a narrow layout
  wants them underneath. Rendering them inside `carousel-shell` forced one
  placement on every section.

  Nested inside the carousel they are found automatically. Anywhere else, pass
  `for:` with the carousel's id and the component resolves it from the
  document — so they can sit before it, after it, or in a sticky bar.

  ## Changing the icon

  `chevron` and `arrow` are directional pairs and the snippet picks the right
  one per side. Any other name from `snippets/icon.liquid` is used for both
  buttons and mirrored on the previous one, which is what you want for a custom
  glyph and means you never ship two SVGs for one arrow.

  `-large` is a suffix, not a glyph: `arrow-large` is the same SVG drawn at a
  bigger fraction of the button. That is why "Large arrows" adds no icon to
  `snippets/icon.liquid` and cannot drift from the small one.

  `none` renders nothing at all — it is the off switch for a merchant who wants
  the pagination to be the only control.

  ## The button box

  Three settings decide what the glyph sits on, and they are deliberately
  separate from the colours:

    carousel_nav_shape         none | circle | square — the "Icon background"
    carousel_nav_border_style  solid | dashed | none
    carousel_nav_border_radius px, and only meaningful on a square

  `none` is a bare glyph: no fill, no border, and the icon keeps the scheme's
  arrow colour rather than the inverted one a filled button uses. It still
  carries the full 44px hit area, because a target that shrinks to the size of
  its ink is a target a thumb misses.

  @param {object} [settings] - Section settings
  @param {string} [carousel] - Carousel element id. Omit when nested inside it.
  @param {string} [icon] - Overrides `settings.carousel_nav_icon`
  @param {string} [position] - outside | inside | bottom | inline
  @param {string} [style] - split | ghost | solid
  @param {number} [size] - Button diameter in px
  @param {string} [shape] - none | circle | square. Overrides the setting.
  @param {string} [border_style] - solid | dashed | none. Overrides the setting.
  @param {number} [radius] - Corner radius in px, squares only.
  @param {string} [classes] - Extra classes

  @example
    {% render 'carousel-nav', settings: section.settings %}
    {% render 'carousel-nav', carousel: carousel_id, position: 'inline', settings: section.settings %}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign icon_name = icon | default: settings.carousel_nav_icon | default: 'chevron'

  # The size suffix is stripped before the name is matched, so `arrow-large`
  # resolves to `arrow-left` / `arrow-right` like `arrow` does and only the
  # ratio below changes. `icon-size` cannot do this — it is the glyph inside a
  # button whose diameter is already a merchant setting.
  assign icon_ratio = 0.4
  if icon_name contains '-large'
    assign icon_name = icon_name | remove: '-large'
    assign icon_ratio = 0.62
  endif

  assign place = position | default: settings.carousel_nav_position | default: 'outside'
  assign btn_style = style | default: settings.carousel_controls_style | default: 'ghost'
  assign btn_size = size | default: settings.carousel_nav_size | default: 44
  assign icon_size = btn_size | times: icon_ratio | round

  assign nav_shape = shape | default: settings.carousel_nav_shape | default: 'circle'
  assign nav_border = border_style | default: settings.carousel_nav_border_style | default: 'solid'

  assign nav_radius = radius | default: settings.carousel_nav_border_radius | default: 8 | plus: 0
  case nav_shape
    when 'square'
      assign radius_value = nav_radius | append: 'px'
    when 'none'
      assign radius_value = '0'
    else
      assign radius_value = '50%'
  endcase

  assign paired = false
  if icon_name == 'chevron' or icon_name == 'arrow'
    assign paired = true
  endif

  if paired
    assign icon_previous = icon_name | append: '-left'
    assign icon_next = icon_name | append: '-right'
  else
    assign icon_previous = icon_name
    assign icon_next = icon_name
  endif

  # `ghost` is the outlined pair, `solid` the filled pair, `split` one of each.
  # Both roles carry a class now: `ghost` used to be the empty string and so
  # rendered as the bare `.carousel-button`, which already paints the scheme's
  # arrow background — three styles that looked like one. See `base.css`.
  case btn_style
    when 'solid'
      assign class_previous = 'carousel-button--solid'
      assign class_next = 'carousel-button--solid'
    when 'split'
      assign class_previous = 'carousel-button--ghost'
      assign class_next = 'carousel-button--solid'
    else
      assign class_previous = 'carousel-button--ghost'
      assign class_next = 'carousel-button--ghost'
  endcase

  unless paired
    assign class_previous = class_previous | append: ' carousel-button--mirror'
  endunless
-%}

{%- unless icon_name == 'none' -%}
  <div
    class="carousel-nav carousel-nav--{{ place }} carousel-nav--bg-{{ nav_shape }} {{ classes }}"
    data-carousel-nav
    style="--carousel-nav-size: {{ btn_size }}px; --carousel-nav-radius: {{ radius_value }}; --carousel-nav-border-style: {{ nav_border }};"
  >
    <button
      type="button"
      class="carousel-button {{ class_previous }}"
      data-ref="previous"
      {% if carousel != blank %}data-carousel-for="{{ carousel }}"{% endif %}
      aria-label="{{ 'accessibility.previous_slide' | t }}"
    >
      {% render 'icon', icon: icon_previous, size: icon_size %}
    </button>

    <button
      type="button"
      class="carousel-button {{ class_next }}"
      data-ref="next"
      {% if carousel != blank %}data-carousel-for="{{ carousel }}"{% endif %}
      aria-label="{{ 'accessibility.next_slide' | t }}"
    >
      {% render 'icon', icon: icon_next, size: icon_size %}
    </button>
  </div>
{%- endunless -%}
